# tools/al/ales_cue_sheet_witness.rish -- the marker track travels as text, a cue sheet (ALES22).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES22.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-fill-ales22-lotus-cue-sheet.md
# Stands on: lotus/markers.rye (ALES20) -- the sorted, unique named track and its one `add` reused whole, so every track bound is
# enforced at the text edge; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip master; tally/parse_int.rye -- strict decimal at the text edge.
#
# lotus/cue_sheet.rye lets a keeper's named places PERSIST and TRAVEL: a whole markers track rendered as a format lotus-cue-sheet-v1
# record, and parsed back into exactly the same sorted, named track -- mirroring HUNK31's render_book / parse_book idiom. The crux: a
# track rendered to a cue sheet parses back to EXACTLY the same track (parse(render(m)) byte-for-byte) and render(parse(render(m)))
# is a fixed point, the parse enforcing every bound through ALES20's one markers.add. The selftest proves: a track renders to the
# exact expected text; parse recovers every position and name; the fixed point holds; a shuffled record parses to the same sorted
# track and a name with a space round-trips; every corruption refuses by name (BadRecord / Duplicate / PastEnd / BadName /
# MarkersFull / Overflow). Purely local -- no network, no keys, no funds.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_cue_sheet_witness.rish

say "ales-cue-sheet: Language EN — ALES22 lets the ALES20 markers track travel as text: a keeper's named places persist and port as a format lotus-cue-sheet-v1 record, the audio world's own word for a list of named time positions."
say "ales-cue-sheet: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: a track rendered to a cue sheet parses back to exactly the same sorted, named track, and render(parse(render(x))) is a fixed point, the render canonical and the parse enforcing every bound through ALES20's one markers.add."
say "ales-cue-sheet: Lens TAME — markers.add reused whole (no new bound, no re-spelled invariant) and parse_int's strict decimal refusing a leading zero at the text edge; every corruption refuses by name; read-only over the master; siloed to lotus/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no meter, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/cue_sheet"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/cue_sheet.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-cue-sheet: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/cue_sheet.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-cue-sheet: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-cue-sheet: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-cue-sheet' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-cue-sheet: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-cue-sheet line"

say "ales-cue-sheet: GREEN — a keeper's marks outlive the session and travel to another hand: a whole markers track renders to a canonical format lotus-cue-sheet-v1 record and parses back byte-for-byte, every bound enforced at the text edge. The next Lotus rung can name a selection (a marked span cut or gained as one), offer the choice of law where a crossfade meets a marked edit, or (a module seam) carry the cue sheet across the ALES0 audio wire as its own framed payload, tying a shared marker map to the Mikrophone's capture."
